{"id": "b5dfdaa5-67ac-4fa9-86c0-ef8e37c912ec", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:55:35.626933", "source": "user", "parent_id": "56ceb100-0344-452a-ab03-e0f6d912f76c", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach looks well-scoped and source-honest. Decisions on the five check-in items before you draft the full design doc, plus a few notes on the assumptions/uncertainties list. (Note: nothing in the supplied material fixes any concrete MDGEN variant, training stride, system choice, dataset source, or compute target — so the design doc should propose specific values with explicit 'researcher-confirmable' labels rather than leave them abstract; do NOT present any proposed default values as if they were cited. Also, the referenced `track.md` does not exist on disk per your inspection — flag this once in the doc and proceed. The phrase 'oscillations in torsion angles' in the question is the only metric-shaped cue supplied; treat every other metric proposal as yours to make and label. Do NOT claim any MDGEN paper, checkpoint, dataset, or result was inspected — none were. Observational note, not a demand: 'MDGEN'-style diffusion/flow bridges across MD-stride gaps is the established idea in the literature, but absent any provided source you may use the concept to motivate the design without making factual claims. The five decisions: (1) System scope — TIERED, with alanine dipeptide as the primary, must-execute tier because it is the cheapest system on which φ/ψ oscillations are physically meaningful and decoupled from one another; a small peptide (e.g., a short unstructured or minimally structured one) as secondary; fast-folding proteins explicitly out of scope in the sandbox but scoped in the design as a third tier contingent on external compute and a supplied checkpoint. (2) Recovery criterion — BOTH, with qualitative shape agreement on C_φ(τ)/C_ψ(τ) and S(ω) as the primary read (presence of the high-frequency peak, correct peak frequency within tolerance, finite integrated power vs. the aliased subsampled baseline) and a secondary quantitative bound on relative error of C(τ) over a defined lag window (e.g., 0–20× the saved-frame stride) and on integrated PSD in the oscillation band. State that meaningful quantitative bounds need enough independent trajectory segments to estimate error bars, and that sample-size estimation is part of the analysis plan. (3) Subsampling treatment — SWEEP over a small set of strides that includes the MDGEN training stride as the anchor, plus at least one finer and one coarser stride, so that degradation as a function of stride is characterizable; ground-truth frame rate must remain fixed across the sweep and must be much shorter than the dominant φ/ψ oscillation period, with a feasibility check that the ground-truth frame rate satisfies this condition before running. (4) Computational scope — design assumes an EXTERNAL execution environment (MDGEN code + checkpoint + reference trajectory supplied by researcher as inputs), with the local sandbox used only for dry feasibility checks (e.g., tiny synthetic two-atom demo of the metric definitions). State this scoping explicitly in the doc and make the 'what must be injected before execution' list a first-class section: MDGEN package and pinned version, checkpoint files, system topology and force-field metadata, high-frame-rate reference trajectory(ies), and chosen stride(s). (5) Negative controls — KEEP all four (linear interpolation, cubic-spline interpolation, time-reversed coarse-frames → MDGEN, shuffled coarse-frames → MDGEN). Their joint inclusion is what isolates MDGEN specifically: linear/spline verify it is not just smooth interpolation, time-reversed/shuffled verify it is not just memorizing two-point distributions. Optionally add a brief MD-relaxation/energy-minimization physics baseline if computationally cheap for the chosen system, but not required. On assumptions A1–A6: accept A1, A2, A4, A5, A6; amend A3 to read that linear and cubic-spline baselines attribute recovery *beyond* smooth geometric interpolation, with the time-reversed and shuffled controls completing the attribution (so attribution is a joint claim across baselines and controls, not a single-baseline claim); add A7 that the ground-truth frame rate is a binding feasibility constraint (oscillation period > saved-frame interval). On uncertainties: proceed with placeholder defaults labelled 'researcher-confirmable' for U1 (variant/checkpoint/training-stride), U2 (alanine primary per decision 1), U3 (external trajectory, frame-rate feasibility check required), U6 (sweep per decision 3); defer U4 and U5 to the researcher's 'must-inject' list and secondary-metric section respectively; treat U7 as a documented gap, not a blocker. Proceed to draft the full experiment design document. Structure it as: rationale → research question restatement as falsifiable claims → hypotheses (primary + secondary + null expectations for each control) → conditions (the three-way comparison + tiered systems + stride sweep) → metrics with concrete mathematical definitions (C_φ(τ), C_ψ(τ), S_φ(ω), S_ψ(ω), oscillation-band integrated power, optional quantitative error bounds) → baselines/negative controls (with the predicted qualitative behavior of each) → analysis plan (per-metric comparison procedure, sample-size sketch, statistical tests for paired time series, pre-registration of what counts as 'recovery') → feasibility checks (binding ground-truth frame-rate constraint, CPU-vs-GPU cost for each tier, dependency footprint) → what the researcher must inject before execution (MDGEN package + version, checkpoint(s), system topology + force field, reference trajectory files with frame-rate metadata, stride list) → explicit non-goals and out-of-scope items (no claim of executed experiment, no citation of MDGEN paper absent provided source, fast-folding tier is contingent). Keep claims grounded; do not invent numbers, checkpoints, paper titles, or dataset names. Report back with the full design doc as a single deliverable.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "1017cec6-b5d2-409f-a0de-0196e64f4e6f", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:57:23.269671", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b5dfdaa5-67ac-4fa9-86c0-ef8e37c912ec", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Does MDGEN accurately recover fast timestep dynamics missing in subsampled MD?\n\n**Status.** This is a design-only document. No experiment has been executed. After every concrete value below, a `[RC]` tag flags it as a researcher-confirmable placeholder; values without `[RC]` are derived from the supplied question, the environment block in the task brief, or the structure of the analysis itself.\n\n**Two source-grounding notes, each flagged once.**\n\n1. The `track.md` file referenced under `documents` in `/workspace/task.json` is **not present on disk** (verified); this is treated as a documented gap, not a blocker. Its expected content, per the placeholder string in `task.json`, is only the meta-statement that the task is design-only and repositories/compute are not required here. No further setup detail has been received from that source.\n2. No MDGEN paper, release, checkpoint, system specification, force field, dataset, or numerical result has been inspected. The concept of a generative diffusion/flow model bridging gaps between coarse MD frames is used here only as a literature-motivated framing of the design, not as a citation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale\n\nSubstrate molecular-dynamics trajectories are typically saved at coarser intervals than the fast dihedral oscillations of the backbone occur on. Backbone φ, ψ torsions oscillate on timescales well below what most \"save-every-N-steps\" MD pipelines record; the result is that for any saved coarse trajectory, the in-segment torsional time series is either missing or aliased, so any analysis of torsional oscillations must somehow infer or generate what is not on disk. The idea behind MDGEN-style generative bridging is that a learned model can fill the missing time-resolution in a way that, in principle, approximates the underlying physical dynamics rather than merely smoothing.\n\nThe question of interest is narrow: *does such bridging actually recover the missing fast oscillation content?* Because the ground-truth dynamics are continuous but the comparison is masked to a coarse observation regime, the experiment must (i) use a ground truth saved at a frame rate far shorter than the dominant oscillation period, so oscillation is recorded rather than aliased; (ii) apply a non-trivial set of baselines that share the same coarse inputs but cannot by construction reproduce fast oscillations, so any recovery is attributable beyond geometric interpolation; and (iii) include controls that share the same coarse *statistics* but destroy the temporal order, so any recovery is attributable beyond two-point distrbution memorization. Torsional autocorrelation and power spectral density are the natural observables because both are direct unbiased readouts of the fast oscillation content and are defined purely in terms of φ, ψ time series at the recovered fine grid.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Research question, restated as falsifiable claims\n\nThe original question decomposes into three falsifiable claims. Each is paired with what would be measured and what would constitute evidence for or against it.\n\n**C1. Oscillation-presence claim.** There exists a band of angular frequencies ω in the high-frequency regime of the torsional PSD — specifically above the Nyquist frequency of the coarse stride — at which the PSD of φ (and analogously ψ) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories has finite, non-noise integrated power, while the corresponding PSD on the subsampled trajectory has none (by Nyquist, the power is absent because the signal is unrecorded, not because it does not exist). *Evidence for C1:* a clearly resolved peak in S_φ(ω) at the same angular frequency as in the ground-truth PSD, with relative-height ratio above a pre-registered threshold `[RC]`. *Evidence against:* the MDGEN PSD in that band is indistinguishable from noise or from the subsampled-aliased baseline.\n\n**C2. Frequency-localization claim.** When C1 holds, the peak frequency of S_φ(ω) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories matches the ground-truth peak frequency within a pre-registered tolerance `[RC]` (the tolerance is set relative to the bandwidth of the ground-truth peak, e.g. ± one FWHM). *Evidence against:* the peak is at a shifted frequency, broader than ground truth by more than `[RC]`, or split into multiple peaks.\n\n**C3. Attribution claim.** Both C1 and C2 hold specifically because of MDGEN's generative structure — not because smooth geometric interpolation between coarse frames happens to look noiseless at fine scales, and not because the model is merely matching marginal or pairwise structural statistics. *Evidence for C3:* the four baselines/controls listed in §6 collectively fail C1 and C2 in the predicted ways (§6.2), while MDGEN passes them. *Evidence against:* any of linear/spline interpolation passes C1 (would indicate MDGEN gives no benefit beyond interpolation) or shuffled MDGEN passes C1 (would indicate the model is not using temporal conditioning).\n\nThe question is judged affirmative only if all three claims hold jointly.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hypotheses\n\n**Primary.**\n- **H1.** *MDGEN-augmented trajectories recover the high-frequency torsional oscillation content of φ and ψ that is absent from subsampled trajectories.*\n\n**Primary nulls (paired with their respective controls).**\n- **H0_lin.** Linear interpolation between coarse frames does **not** recover the oscillation band (§6.2.1).\n- **H0_spl.** Cubic-spline interpolation does **not** recover the oscillation band (§6.2.2).\n- **H0_rev.** MDGEN run on time-reversed coarse frames has the same coarse-frame joint statistics and the same marginal φ, ψ distributions but, to the extent that MDGEN encodes genuine forward-time dynamics, its oscillation-band PSD should differ from forward-time MDGEN (predicted signature in §6.2.3).\n- **H0_shf.** MDGEN run on coarse frames shuffled in time has the same marginal φ, ψ statistics but losstemporal structure; predicted to fail C1 in the oscillation band (§6.2.4).\n\n**Secondary.**\n- **H2.** *Degradation of C1/C2 coverage as the coarse stride increases is graceful rather than catastrophic*, so that at strides below and at the MDGEN training stride recovery holds, and the failure pattern is monotone in stride.\n- **H3.** *Overall torsional free-energy structure is preserved by MDGEN augmentation.* The Ramachandran free energy F(φ, ψ) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories agrees with ground truth on the populated regions within a pre-registered tolerance `[RC]`.\n\n**Negative expectation that must be checked.**\n- **N1.** *MDGEN augmentation does not artificially flatten fast oscillations.* If MDGEN were producing frames that look \"diffused\" at the fine scale, C1 would spuriously appear to fail while C3 might still pass.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Conditions\n\n### 4.1 Three-way primary comparison\n\nFor each trajectory segment of length L at the ground-truth frame rate Δt_gt, the experiment produces three parallel fine-rate time series at the same effective frame rate:\n\n1. **Ground truth.** φ, ψ extracted from the original fine-rate trajectory at Δt_gt.\n2. **Subsampled.** Coarse frames taken every N · Δt_gt frames (N from the stride sweep, §4.3); within each coarse interval, φ, ψ values at the fine grid are *not* available — by construction they would have to be inferred.\n3. **MDGEN-augmented.** The coarse frames (every N · Δt_gt frames) are passed to MDGEN; MDGEN samples the in-between frames; φ, ψ are extracted from the resulting fine-rate trajectory at Δt_aug = Δt_gt (so the same downstream metrics can be applied). This is the strict, replicable recipe; alternative choices (different Δt_aug, different conditioning density) are deferred to the researcher's discretion.\n\n### 4.2 Tiered systems\n\n| Tier | System | Role | In-sandbox? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **T1 (primary, must-execute)** | Alanine dipeptide (ACE-ALA-NME or equivalent capped alanine, vacuum or explicit solvent per study convention `[RC]`) | Cheapest system on which φ, ψ oscillations are physically meaningful and (in vacuum/low friction) effectively decoupled. Acts as the gatekeeper for the methodology. | Yes, with the feasibility checks in §8. |\n| **T2 (secondary)** | A short unstructured or minimally structured peptide `[RC]` (e.g., a 3–10 residue peptide without stable secondary structure) | Adds the effect of neighboring-residue couplings on φ/ψ dynamics; cheap enough on CPU. | Yes. |\n| **T3 (contingent)** | One or more fast-folding proteins (specific identity `[RC]`) | Realistic-system test if external compute and a checkpoint are provided. | **Out of sandbox**; design is scoped but execution is contingent on §9 inputs. |\n\nAll three tiers test the same falsifiable claims, C1–C3, so a positive result that survives across tiers is the intended robustness criterion.\n\n### 4.3 Stride sweep\n\n- **Anchor.** The MDGEN training stride `[RC]` (kept nominal until the checkpoint is supplied). One full sweep point at this stride.\n- **Finer.** At least one stride strictly less than the anchor, e.g., anchor/[RC] e.g., × 1/10 `[RC]`. Confirms behaviour as stride tightens toward the training distribution.\n- **Coarser.** At least one stride strictly greater than the anchor, e.g., anchor × [RC] e.g., × 10 `[RC]`. Confirms graceful or catastrophic degradation.\n- **Ground-truth frame rate Δt_gt.** Held fixed across the entire sweep and **must satisfy the binding feasibility constraint of §8.1** before any data acquisition. Placeholder value: 1 fs, i.e., oscillation period > a comfortable multiple of saved-frame interval `[RC]`.\n\nThe sweep is the experimental substrate for **H2**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Metrics\n\nNotation used throughout. Let φ_t denote the φ value at frame index t on the *fine-rate* time series of any of the three conditions; μ_φ = ⟨φ_t⟩, σ_φ² = ⟨(φ_t − μ_φ)²⟩ (sample means/variances over a trajectory segment). All metrics are computed per independent trajectory segment, then aggregated (see §7).\n\n### 5.1 Torsional time series at the fine grid\n\nInputs: for each segment of the ground-truth trajectory, the tor t∈{0,…,T−1}. For the subsampled condition, only φ_{t_k} at k=0,1,…,T/N are observed; the \"fine grid\" values at intermediate t are undefined by construction and an attempt to apply the metrics below to subsampled alone is a Nyquist violation that the experiment labels as such. For MDGEN-augmented, φ_t at every fine frame is the dihedral of the MDGEN-sampled structure; bonds/backbones are wrapped using whatever convention MDGEN adopts (i.e., dihedral angle convention consistent with the reference MD engine).\n\n### 5.2 Autocorrelation (primary)\n\nFor each torsional series φ_t (and analogously ψ_t) on the fine grid,\n\n```\nC_φ(τ) = ⟨(φ_t − μ_φ)(φ_{t+τ} − μ_φ)⟩ / σ_φ²,  τ = 0, 1, …, τ_max\n```\n\nwhere ⟨·⟩ is a time average (and for each segment, separately normalized by that segment's σ_φ). Per-segment estimates are computed; cross-segment means and block-bootstrap confidence bands are reported per §7. Reporting points: τ = 0 (≡1, sanity), τ at the *ground-truth* oscillation half-period, τ equal to the coarse stride N·Δt_gt (a regime where subsampled alone carries no information between coarse frames), and τ up to ~20× the coarse stride.\n\n### 5.3 Power spectral density (primary)\n\nS_φ(ω) is the periodogram of φ_t − μ_φ computed on each segment:\n\n```\nS_φ(ω_k) = |Σ_t (φ_t − μ_φ) e^{−i ω_k t}|² / (T · Δt_aug · σ_φ²),   ω_k = 2π k / (T Δt_aug)\n```\n\nwith frequency normalization by σ_φ² to make the spectrum dimensionless and comparable across segments. Welch-style segment-averaging (Welch window `[RC]`, segment overlap `[RC]`) is used per segment; cross-segment averaging is then over the resulting per-segment spectra.\n\n### 5.4 Oscillation-band integrated power (primary)\n\nDefinition of the band is data-driven and pre-registered: from the *ground-truth* spectrum S^gt_φ(ω), identify the dominant high-frequency peak above a frequency floor f_min `[RC]` Hz (chosen to lie clearly above the coarse-stride Nyquist frequency), and take the band as ω ∈ [ω_peak − Δ, ω_peak + Δ] where Δ equals one ground-truth FWHM `[RC]` (or an absolute width `[RC]`, whichever is easier to bound). Integrated power in the band is\n\n```\nP_φ = ∫_{band} S_φ(ω) dω.\n```\n\nThe per-condition band-integrated power ratio R_φ = P_φ^{condition} / P_φ^{ground} is the headline number for **C1**.\n\n### 5.5 Quantitative lag-window bounds (secondary, paired with qualitative)\n\nChoose a lag window τ ∈ [0, τ_window] where τ_window = [RC] × 20 · N · Δt_gt `[RC]`. Define\n\n```\nε_φ = (1/|W|) · ∫_{τ∈W} |C_φ^{condition}(τ) − C_φ^{gt}(τ)| dτ\n```\n\nand report the ratio (or relative L1) ε_φ / ‖C_φ^{gt}‖_1. Same for ψ. Pre-registered acceptable bound: ε_φ below `[RC]`. This is the quantitative counterpart of §2 C1/C2 and is the operationalization of \"shape agreement within tolerance\" required by the qualitative metric.\n\n### 5.6 Ramachandran free energy (population-level sanity, **H3**)\n\n`F(φ, ψ) = −kT ln P(φ, ψ)`, with P estimated by 2-D histogram binning on the fine grid over a trajectory segment (per-condition, per-segment). Reported as F-condition vs. F-gt over the densely populated region (defined as the set of (φ, ψ) bins where the ground-truth probability is above a threshold `[RC]`). This is a stratification check and is **explicitly not** a fast-dynamics metric — the brief calls it out as a population-level sanity only.\n\n### 5.7 Time-reverse bispectrum (control-supporting, optional)\n\nFor systems where forward/reverse dynamics might still be detectable, a bispectrum of φ and ψ can flag lost time-asymmetry. Optional, de-prioritized if the autocorrelation evidence is already conclusive.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Baselines and negative controls\n\n### 6.1 Baselines (predict smooth interpolation, must fail C1)\n\n#### 6.2.1 Linear interpolation (LI)\n\nConstruction: between coarse frames φ_{t_k} and φ_{t_{k+1}}, φ_t = φ_{t_k} + (φ_{t_{k+1}} − φ_{t_k})(t − t_k)/(t_{k+1} − t_k). Effective fine-rate φ at every integer frame t. Applied to φ and ψ.\n\n**Predicted behaviour.** C_φ(τ) within any one coarse interval is exactly the linear segment's autocorrelation: a single-peaked, segment-monotone shape with no high-frequency oscillation. P_φ in the oscillation band → 0 (or to a numerical-floor noise level determined by the variance across coarse intervals). Test: LI should fail C1 by a wide margin; that margin is the headroom MDGEN must exceed.\n\n#### 6.2.2 Cubic-spline interpolation (CI)\n\nConstruction: piecewise cubic spline through coarse φ values, with derivative boundary conditions per the chosen convention (natural or not-a-knot `[RC]`).\n\n**Predicted behaviour.** Within a coarse interval, spline interpolation produces a single smooth segment with at most one local extremum; no oscillation. Marginal improvement over LI in mean squared error, no genuine improvement on oscillation-band power. Test: CI should also fail C1, and the LI-vs-CI gap should be small in the oscillation band (because both fail). This is the joint claim of §6 — both fail, but they fail differently at low frequencies.\n\n### 6.3 Generative controls (predict forward-dynamics-sensitive vs not)\n\n#### 6.3.3 Time-reversed coarse frames → MDGEN (REV)\n\nConstruction: take the coarse frames in original order t_0, t_1, …, t_K. Form the reversed sequence t_K, t_{K−1}, …, t_0, and run MDGEN bridging on it. The joint distribution of individual coarse frames is identical to the forward case; the temporal order is reversed.\n\n**Predicted behaviour.** C_φ(τ) is by construction symmetric in τ (C_φ(τ) = C_φ(−τ)), so REV's autocorrelation at positive lags will approximately match the forward MDGEN's. Therefore REV is only a *weak* control on autocorrelation. **Honest limitation documented:** REV is included for completeness and as a joint check with the optional bispectrum (§5.7); it should not be relied upon in isolation. The relevant output of this control is to confirm that the primary metric is symmetric in time-reversal (a sanity check that the metric is well-defined).\n\n#### 6.3.4 Shuffled coarse frames → MDGEN (SHF)\n\nConstruction: randomly permute the coarse frame indices; pass the shuffled sequence to MDGEN as if it were a real trajectory.\n\n**Predicted behaviour.** Coarse-frame marginal and pairwise statistics are preserved by permutation; the per-segment power spectral density of MDGEN output on a *shuffled* coarse conditioning is predicted to lose definition because MDGEN's bridging depends on consecutive coarse frames. P_φ in the oscillation band is predicted to be lower than forward MDGEN if MDGEN genuinely uses temporal conditioning (some residual may remain if MDGEN regenerates fine frames conditioned only on a single coarse frame's neighborhood). Test: SHF must fail C1 to confirm that MDGEN is using the temporal order; otherwise §2 C3's \"not memorization\" claim is unsupported.\n\n### 6.4 Optional: physics baseline (deferred)\n\nA deterministic MD-relaxation or energy minimization between consecutive coarse frames, using the supplied force field. System-dependent cost; for alanine dipeptide it is cheap. Predicted behaviour: produces a smooth relaxation trajectory that should fail C1 by the same logic as LI/CI (no oscillation). Included only if cost is negligible.\n\n### 6.5 Attribution logic (updated A3)\n\nNo single baseline or control is dispositive. The four baselines/controls are evaluated jointly:\n\n- LI and CI together confirm that recovery is *not* a free consequence of any smooth interpolation.\n- SHF confirms recovery is *not* a free consequence of matching marginal or pairwise structural statistics.\n- REV is a sanity check that the primary metric is time-symmetric where it should be.\n\nC3 (the attribution claim) is judged to hold only if **MDGEN passes C1–C2 while LI, CI, and SHF each fail C1** (REV is allowed to behave like MDGEN on autocorrelation, as expected).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis plan\n\n### 7.1 Per-metric comparison procedure\n\nFor each trajectory system × stride × independent segment, compute φ, ψ spect tra at the fine grid, derive C_φ(τ), C_ψ(τ), S_φ(ω), S_ψ(ω), P_φ, P_ψ, ε_φ, ε_ψ, F(φ, ψ), and per-condition band ratios. Aggregate across segments by computing per-system, per-stride sample mean and block-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals.\n\n### 7.2 Sample-size sketch\n\nPer-segment variabilities of C_φ(τ), S_φ(ω), and P_φ are unknown *a priori*, since no claimable reference numbers exist here. Sketch: for a chosen-effect-on-band-power Δ = (P_φ^{gen} − P_φ^{shuf}) / σ, with σ estimated from a pilot of `[RC]` independent segments per tier, the standard paired-difference formula at two-sided α = 0.05, power 0.8, gives a per-tier sample size N_seg that is part of the analysis output; the design treats this as an analytic computation rather than a pre-committed number. Sections §7.3 and §7.4 turn the sample-size estimate into the actual pre-registration.\n\n### 7.3 Statistical tests for paired time series\n\n- *Per-lag, per-physical-pair comparison of C_φ(τ):* paired block bootstrap (block = coarse-interval for autocorrelation; block = segment for PSD) to produce 95% CIs on the mean difference; report the lag-window CIs as the operationalization of ε.\n- *PSD-band power:* paired bootstrap on per-segment integrated power (P_φ^{condition} − P_φ^{gt}); same for ψ.\n- *Frequency-localization (C2):* paired bootstrap on the peak frequency ω_peak derived from each segment's spectrum; the tolerance `[RC]` is reported as a CI on (ω_peak^{gen} − ω_peak^{gt}).\n- *Ramachandran (H3):* paired bootstrap on per-binned log-probability differences over the populated bin mask.\n\nTests are paired because each MDGEN-augmented segment has a matched ground-truth segment (same trajectory origin, different bridging), so the paired structure is automatic.\n\n### 7.4 Pre-registration of what counts as \"recovery\"\n\nA condition × system × stride is judged to **recover** fast torsional dynamics jointly and only if all the following hold on the per-segment paired estimates:\n\n1. *C1 — oscillation presence:* the lower 95% CI on R_φ = P_φ^{gen} / P_φ^{gt} in the oscillation band is above `[RC]` (e.g., fraction of ground-truth integrated power) **and** the lower 95% CI on R_φ − R_φ^{LI} is positive in the same band (i.e., MDGEN exceeds linear interpolation).\n2. *C2 — frequency localization:* the 95% CI on the difference ω_peak^{gen} − ω_peak^{gt} contains 0 and is contained within ± `[RC]` FWHM (or absolute tolerance).\n3. *C3 — attribution:* LI, CI, and SHF each individually show lower-bound CIs on their R· below `[RC]` in the oscillation band, while MDGEN satisfies (1). REV is reported separately for the symmetry sanity check.\n\nAcross tiers the design declares an affirmative answer to the original question if C1–C3 hold at the anchor stride on T1 (alanine dipeptide, primary) and at minimum on T2; T3 is reported as a contingent extension.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility checks (gates before execution)\n\n### 8.1 Binding ground-truth frame-rate constraint (added A7)\n\nPre-execution check, must pass before any data acquisition. Compute φ, ψ spectrograms on a short pilot of the ground-truth trajectory at the candidate Δt_gt and identify the dominant torsional oscillation period T_osc. Pass if T_osc > `[RC]` × Δt_gt (e.g., × 20, comfortably above Nyquist and above any minimum sampling for oscillation frequency resolution). Fail otherwise; in which case the study is not answerable on that dataset at that Δt_gt and a finer-Δt_gt reference is required. Documented as a hard gate rather than a tunable parameter.\n\n### 8.2 CPU-vs-GPU cost per tier\n\n- **T1 (alanine).** Per coarse-stride fine-rate trajectory segment of length L_gt `[RC]` ps, MDGEN sampling `[RC]` frames between consecutive coarse anchors. Approximate order-of-magnitude cost in CPU-hours/segment is `[RC]` (e.g., 0.1–10) at the most inexpensive `MDGEN` setting consistent with §9. Aggregate across the stride sweep and `[RC]` segments becomes a wall-clock plan;\n- **T2 (peptide).** Cost scales roughly with the number of atoms; within reach of the sandbox at `[RC]` segments per stride (TBC after benchmark in §8.3 below);\n- **T3 (protein).** Out-of-sandbox; execution requires external compute and the §9 inputs.\n\n### 8.3 Dependency footprint and benchmarking plan\n\nLocal feasibility check (per the researcher's scoping decision): a *tiny synthetic two-atom demo* of the metric definitions (LI, CI, autocorrelation, PSD, band-integrated power, paired bootstrap) on simulated noisy torsions, to confirm that the analysis pipeline produces matching C1/C2 results against a known closed-form oscillation + noise mixture. This is the only computation the sandbox is required to execute and is independent of MDGEN availability. Cost: minutes.\n\n### 8.4 Other gating checks\n\n- Trajectory length sufficient for PSD resolution at the lowest ω of interest (FFT resolution Δω = 2π / (T Δt_aug); requirement: oscillation-peak FWHM ≥ `[RC]` × Δω).\n- Segment independence assumption (decorrelation of φ, ψ across segments over the longest τ_window sampled); if violated, increase segment length or shorten τ_window.\n- Per-condition sample-size estimate (§7.2) returns a feasible number of segments per tier.\n\n---\n\n## 9. What the researcher must inject before execution\n\nThis section is first-class: the design cannot move to execution without all of the following.\n\n| Item | Reason | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| MDGEN package + pinned version | The model is the central object under test. | Specific package + commit/version tag `[RC]`. |\n| Checkpoint file(s) | Required to actually run inference; \"MDGEN\" without a checkpoint is undefined. | One per tier; pinned repo + sha `[RC]`. |\n| System topology + force-field metadata | Defines atoms, bonds, and dihedral convention used for φ, ψ extraction. | AMBER/CHARMM/OpenMM/... `[RC]`. |\n| High-frame-rate reference trajectory(ies) with frame-rate metadata | Needed for C1 ground truth. | Δt_gt must pass §8.1; T1, T2 mandatory; T3 contingent. |\n| Chosen stride list | The §4.3 sweep needs concrete values tied to the checkpoint. | Anchor = training stride of the supplied checkpoint `[RC]`; finer and coarser as proposed in §4.3 `[RC]`. |\n| (Implicit) Scientific Python stack + MD analysis lib + pinned versions | Required by §5 metric pipeline. | mdtraj / MDAnalysis / numpy / scipy `[RC]`. |\n\nIf any of T1/T2/T3 cannot be supplied, only the supplied tiers are analysed and that constraint is reported with the results.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Explicit non-goals and out-of-scope items\n\n- **No claim of executed experiment.** This document does not assert that any segment has been computed, that any check has passed, that any MDGEN checkpoint has been loaded, or that any number has been measured.\n- **No MDGEN paper / checkpoint / dataset citation.** No paper title, no checkpoint id, no dataset name, no result is cited. The phrase \"MDGEN-style diffusion/flow bridging\" is used as a literature concept only.\n- **T3 (fast-folding proteins) is contingent.** Designed but not executable in the sandbox; reported only if §9 inputs and external compute are available.\n- **No claim of which specific numerical recovery threshold is \"good enough.\"** All thresholds `[RC]` are pre-registration placeholders, not cited standards.\n- **Out of scope:** whether the recovered fast dynamics are *physically meaningful for downstream observables* (e.g., NMR J-coupling, FRET, infrared spectra inferred from trajectories). These are downstream scientific-application questions that the present design does not adjudicate. Karplus-type J-coupling reconstruction from MDGEN-augmented φ distributions is flagged as an exploratory downstream test only, not part of the primary metric battery.\n- **Out of scope: training, fine-tuning, or retraining of MDGEN.** The design treats the model as a fixed black box defined by its supplied checkpoint.\n- **Out of scope: comparisons to alternative generative models** (other diffusion / flow / score-based bridges, or other post-hoc upsamplers) unless the researcher explicitly adds them; only the four baselines/controls specified in §6 are included by default.\n- **Out of scope: the absent `track.md` content.** Flagged once in §0; design proceeds without it.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A — Assumption ledger (post-decision)\n\n- **A1.** \"Fast dynamics\" is operationalized as backbone φ, ψ torsional oscillations on timescales much smaller than the MDGEN training stride. *Accepted.*\n- **A2.** φ, ψ autocorrelation functions and their power spectral densities on the recovered fine grid are the primary, falsifiable probes; Ramachandran free energy is a population-level sanity check only. *Accepted.*\n- **A3.** *Amended (per researcher):* attribution of any recovery is a *joint* claim across baselines and controls — specifically, MDGEN passes C1–C2 while LI, CI, and SHF each fail C1 in oscillation-band power; the joint pattern is the attribution. REV is a weaker sanity check because torsion autocorrelation is approximately symmetric under time reversal.\n- **A4.** Time-reversed and shuffled-frame MDGEN augmentations are sufficient (jointly with LI/CI) to rule out smooth-interpolation and two-point-distribution-memorization explanations of any recovery. *Accepted, with the joint-claim caveat.*\n- **A5.** The design document is feasible for alanine dipeptide as a primary tier in the provided 24-CPU / no-GPU sandbox; larger systems are scoped but flagged as out-of-sandbox. *Accepted.*\n- **A6.** The supplied material fixes only the research question and the design-only framing; all concrete MDGEN/system/checkpoint/metric choices are proposals labelled `[RC]`, not citations of provided source. *Accepted.*\n- **A7 (added).** The ground-truth frame rate Δt_gt is a binding feasibility constraint: the dominant φ, ψ oscillation period must be larger than the saved-frame interval by a comfortable multiple `[RC]` (typically ≥ ~20×), checked before data acquisition as a hard gate. *Accepted.*\n\n## Appendix B — Uncertainty ledger (post-decision)\n\n- **U1.** MDGEN variant, checkpoint, training stride — `[RC]` placeholders only; deferred to §9 must-inject list.\n- **U2.** Evaluation systems — T1 alanine dipeptide (primary, gating), T2 short unstructured peptide (secondary), T3 fast-folding proteins (contingent), per the researcher's decision.\n- **U3.** Ground-truth trajectory source — external trajectory supplied by researcher; §8.1 binding frame-rate check must pass before any other analysis.\n- **U4.** Compute target — execution assumed in an external environment (§9 must-inject); local sandbox used only for the §8.3 synthetic metric-pipeline sanity check.\n- **U5.** Quantitative recovery tolerance — thresholds `[RC]` in §7.4 are pre-registration placeholders, refined during analysis once per-segment variability is estimated from a pilot.\n- **U6.** Subsampling regime — sweep (anchor + finer + coarser strides), per the researcher's decision.\n- **U7.** Documented absence of `track.md` — flagged once in §0; **not a blocker**, treated as a known gap.", "evidence": "Workspace = `/workspace/task.json` only (verified by `find /workspace -maxdepth 4 -type f`). Contents parsed: question, context that the task is design-only and \"do not claim that an experiment was run\", and a `documents.track.md` placeholder whose only text is `\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` That `track.md` file is not on disk (verified by `find / -maxdepth 3 -iname track.md`, no results). No MDGEN-named artefacts anywhere on the filesystem (`find / -maxdepth 3 -iname \"*mdgen*\"` no results; no alanine / peptide / trajectory files anywhere; `/home/actor/` empty; `/tmp`, `/data`, `/opt` empty of relevant material). Environment block from the task brief pins: bubblewrap runtime, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24, datasets=[], python=python3, deps not preinstalled. The phrase \"oscillations in torsion angles\" in the supplied question is the only metric-shaped cue. All other metric, system, checkpoint, stride, and threshold choices in the design are explicitly flagged `[RC]` (researcher-confirmable placeholders), per the researcher's instruction; none is presented with paper-style citation. No claim of any executed experiment, paper, checkpoint, dataset, or numerical result is made.", "assumptions": ["A1. 'Fast dynamics' operationalized as backbone φ/ψ torsional oscillations on timescales much smaller than the MDGEN training stride. (Accepted by researcher.)", "A2. φ/ψ autocorrelation C(τ) and PSD S(ω) on the recovered fine grid are primary falsifiable probes; Ramachandran F(φ,ψ) is a population-level sanity check only. (Accepted.)", "A3 (amended). Attribution of recovery is a joint claim across baselines and controls — MDGEN passes C1–C2 while LI, CI, SHF each fail C1; REV is a weaker symmetry-sanity check because torsional autocorrelation is approximately symmetric under time reversal. (Per researcher's amendment.)", "A4. Time-reversed and shuffled-frame MDGEN augmentations are sufficient (jointly with LI/CI) to rule out smooth-interpolation and two-point-distribution-memorization explanations. (Accepted with joint-claim caveat.)", "A5. The design document is feasible for alanine dipeptide as a primary tier in the provided 24-CPU / no-GPU sandbox; larger systems scoped but flagged out-of-sandbox. (Accepted.)", "A6. The supplied material fixes only the research question and the design-only framing; all concrete MDGEN/system/checkpoint/metric choices are proposals labelled [RC], not citations of provided source. (Accepted.)", "A7 (added). Ground-truth saved-frame interval Δt_gt is a binding feasibility constraint: dominant φ/ψ oscillation period > ~[RC]× Δt_gt, checked before any data acquisition as a hard gate. (Added per researcher's request.)"], "uncertainties": ["U1. MDGEN variant, checkpoint, training stride — [RC] placeholders only, deferred to §9 must-inject list.", "U2. Evaluation systems — T1 alanine dipeptide primary (gating), T2 short peptide secondary, T3 fast-folding proteins contingent, per researcher's decision.", "U3. Ground-truth trajectory source — external, supplied by researcher; §8.1 binding frame-rate check must pass first.", "U4. Compute target — external execution environment for MDGEN inference; sandbox only for §8.3 synthetic metric-pipeline sanity check.", "U5. Quantitative recovery tolerance — [RC] pre-registration placeholders, refined post-pilot per §7.4.", "U6. Subsampling regime — anchor + finer + coarser stride sweep, per researcher's decision.", "U7. Referenced track.md is absent from disk; flagged once in §0; treated as a documented gap, not a blocker."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-1c430436-5951-409e-a9e0-2d5503eec3ef", "tool_call": {"id": "call-1c430436-5951-409e-a9e0-2d5503eec3ef", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full experiment design delivered (design-only, source-honest)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Does MDGEN accurately recover fast timestep dynamics missing in subsampled MD?\\n\\n**Status.** This is a design-only document. No experiment has been executed. After every concrete value below, a `[RC]` tag flags it as a researcher-confirmable placeholder; values without `[RC]` are derived from the supplied question, the environment block in the task brief, or the structure of the analysis itself.\\n\\n**Two source-grounding notes, each flagged once.**\\n\\n1. The `track.md` file referenced under `documents` in `/workspace/task.json` is **not present on disk** (verified); this is treated as a documented gap, not a blocker. Its expected content, per the placeholder string in `task.json`, is only the meta-statement that the task is design-only and repositories/compute are not required here. No further setup detail has been received from that source.\\n2. No MDGEN paper, release, checkpoint, system specification, force field, dataset, or numerical result has been inspected. The concept of a generative diffusion/flow model bridging gaps between coarse MD frames is used here only as a literature-motivated framing of the design, not as a citation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Rationale\\n\\nSubstrate molecular-dynamics trajectories are typically saved at coarser intervals than the fast dihedral oscillations of the backbone occur on. Backbone \\u03c6, \\u03c8 torsions oscillate on timescales well below what most \\\"save-every-N-steps\\\" MD pipelines record; the result is that for any saved coarse trajectory, the in-segment torsional time series is either missing or aliased, so any analysis of torsional oscillations must somehow infer or generate what is not on disk. The idea behind MDGEN-style generative bridging is that a learned model can fill the missing time-resolution in a way that, in principle, approximates the underlying physical dynamics rather than merely smoothing.\\n\\nThe question of interest is narrow: *does such bridging actually recover the missing fast oscillation content?* Because the ground-truth dynamics are continuous but the comparison is masked to a coarse observation regime, the experiment must (i) use a ground truth saved at a frame rate far shorter than the dominant oscillation period, so oscillation is recorded rather than aliased; (ii) apply a non-trivial set of baselines that share the same coarse inputs but cannot by construction reproduce fast oscillations, so any recovery is attributable beyond geometric interpolation; and (iii) include controls that share the same coarse *statistics* but destroy the temporal order, so any recovery is attributable beyond two-point distrbution memorization. Torsional autocorrelation and power spectral density are the natural observables because both are direct unbiased readouts of the fast oscillation content and are defined purely in terms of \\u03c6, \\u03c8 time series at the recovered fine grid.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Research question, restated as falsifiable claims\\n\\nThe original question decomposes into three falsifiable claims. Each is paired with what would be measured and what would constitute evidence for or against it.\\n\\n**C1. Oscillation-presence claim.** There exists a band of angular frequencies \\u03c9 in the high-frequency regime of the torsional PSD \\u2014 specifically above the Nyquist frequency of the coarse stride \\u2014 at which the PSD of \\u03c6 (and analogously \\u03c8) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories has finite, non-noise integrated power, while the corresponding PSD on the subsampled trajectory has none (by Nyquist, the power is absent because the signal is unrecorded, not because it does not exist). *Evidence for C1:* a clearly resolved peak in S_\\u03c6(\\u03c9) at the same angular frequency as in the ground-truth PSD, with relative-height ratio above a pre-registered threshold `[RC]`. *Evidence against:* the MDGEN PSD in that band is indistinguishable from noise or from the subsampled-aliased baseline.\\n\\n**C2. Frequency-localization claim.** When C1 holds, the peak frequency of S_\\u03c6(\\u03c9) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories matches the ground-truth peak frequency within a pre-registered tolerance `[RC]` (the tolerance is set relative to the bandwidth of the ground-truth peak, e.g. \\u00b1 one FWHM). *Evidence against:* the peak is at a shifted frequency, broader than ground truth by more than `[RC]`, or split into multiple peaks.\\n\\n**C3. Attribution claim.** Both C1 and C2 hold specifically because of MDGEN's generative structure \\u2014 not because smooth geometric interpolation between coarse frames happens to look noiseless at fine scales, and not because the model is merely matching marginal or pairwise structural statistics. *Evidence for C3:* the four baselines/controls listed in \\u00a76 collectively fail C1 and C2 in the predicted ways (\\u00a76.2), while MDGEN passes them. *Evidence against:* any of linear/spline interpolation passes C1 (would indicate MDGEN gives no benefit beyond interpolation) or shuffled MDGEN passes C1 (would indicate the model is not using temporal conditioning).\\n\\nThe question is judged affirmative only if all three claims hold jointly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Hypotheses\\n\\n**Primary.**\\n- **H1.** *MDGEN-augmented trajectories recover the high-frequency torsional oscillation content of \\u03c6 and \\u03c8 that is absent from subsampled trajectories.*\\n\\n**Primary nulls (paired with their respective controls).**\\n- **H0_lin.** Linear interpolation between coarse frames does **not** recover the oscillation band (\\u00a76.2.1).\\n- **H0_spl.** Cubic-spline interpolation does **not** recover the oscillation band (\\u00a76.2.2).\\n- **H0_rev.** MDGEN run on time-reversed coarse frames has the same coarse-frame joint statistics and the same marginal \\u03c6, \\u03c8 distributions but, to the extent that MDGEN encodes genuine forward-time dynamics, its oscillation-band PSD should differ from forward-time MDGEN (predicted signature in \\u00a76.2.3).\\n- **H0_shf.** MDGEN run on coarse frames shuffled in time has the same marginal \\u03c6, \\u03c8 statistics but losstemporal structure; predicted to fail C1 in the oscillation band (\\u00a76.2.4).\\n\\n**Secondary.**\\n- **H2.** *Degradation of C1/C2 coverage as the coarse stride increases is graceful rather than catastrophic*, so that at strides below and at the MDGEN training stride recovery holds, and the failure pattern is monotone in stride.\\n- **H3.** *Overall torsional free-energy structure is preserved by MDGEN augmentation.* The Ramachandran free energy F(\\u03c6, \\u03c8) on MDGEN-augmented trajectories agrees with ground truth on the populated regions within a pre-registered tolerance `[RC]`.\\n\\n**Negative expectation that must be checked.**\\n- **N1.** *MDGEN augmentation does not artificially flatten fast oscillations.* If MDGEN were producing frames that look \\\"diffused\\\" at the fine scale, C1 would spuriously appear to fail while C3 might still pass.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Conditions\\n\\n### 4.1 Three-way primary comparison\\n\\nFor each trajectory segment of length L at the ground-truth frame rate \\u0394t_gt, the experiment produces three parallel fine-rate time series at the same effective frame rate:\\n\\n1. **Ground truth.** \\u03c6, \\u03c8 extracted from the original fine-rate trajectory at \\u0394t_gt.\\n2. **Subsampled.** Coarse frames taken every N \\u00b7 \\u0394t_gt frames (N from the stride sweep, \\u00a74.3); within each coarse interval, \\u03c6, \\u03c8 values at the fine grid are *not* available \\u2014 by construction they would have to be inferred.\\n3. **MDGEN-augmented.** The coarse frames (every N \\u00b7 \\u0394t_gt frames) are passed to MDGEN; MDGEN samples the in-between frames; \\u03c6, \\u03c8 are extracted from the resulting fine-rate trajectory at \\u0394t_aug = \\u0394t_gt (so the same downstream metrics can be applied). This is the strict, replicable recipe; alternative choices (different \\u0394t_aug, different conditioning density) are deferred to the researcher's discretion.\\n\\n### 4.2 Tiered systems\\n\\n| Tier | System | Role | In-sandbox? |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **T1 (primary, must-execute)** | Alanine dipeptide (ACE-ALA-NME or equivalent capped alanine, vacuum or explicit solvent per study convention `[RC]`) | Cheapest system on which \\u03c6, \\u03c8 oscillations are physically meaningful and (in vacuum/low friction) effectively decoupled. Acts as the gatekeeper for the methodology. | Yes, with the feasibility checks in \\u00a78. |\\n| **T2 (secondary)** | A short unstructured or minimally structured peptide `[RC]` (e.g., a 3\\u201310 residue peptide without stable secondary structure) | Adds the effect of neighboring-residue couplings on \\u03c6/\\u03c8 dynamics; cheap enough on CPU. | Yes. |\\n| **T3 (contingent)** | One or more fast-folding proteins (specific identity `[RC]`) | Realistic-system test if external compute and a checkpoint are provided. | **Out of sandbox**; design is scoped but execution is contingent on \\u00a79 inputs. |\\n\\nAll three tiers test the same falsifiable claims, C1\\u2013C3, so a positive result that survives across tiers is the intended robustness criterion.\\n\\n### 4.3 Stride sweep\\n\\n- **Anchor.** The MDGEN training stride `[RC]` (kept nominal until the checkpoint is supplied). One full sweep point at this stride.\\n- **Finer.** At least one stride strictly less than the anchor, e.g., anchor/[RC] e.g., \\u00d7 1/10 `[RC]`. Confirms behaviour as stride tightens toward the training distribution.\\n- **Coarser.** At least one stride strictly greater than the anchor, e.g., anchor \\u00d7 [RC] e.g., \\u00d7 10 `[RC]`. Confirms graceful or catastrophic degradation.\\n- **Ground-truth frame rate \\u0394t_gt.** Held fixed across the entire sweep and **must satisfy the binding feasibility constraint of \\u00a78.1** before any data acquisition. Placeholder value: 1 fs, i.e., oscillation period > a comfortable multiple of saved-frame interval `[RC]`.\\n\\nThe sweep is the experimental substrate for **H2**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Metrics\\n\\nNotation used throughout. Let \\u03c6_t denote the \\u03c6 value at frame index t on the *fine-rate* time series of any of the three conditions; \\u03bc_\\u03c6 = \\u27e8\\u03c6_t\\u27e9, \\u03c3_\\u03c6\\u00b2 = \\u27e8(\\u03c6_t \\u2212 \\u03bc_\\u03c6)\\u00b2\\u27e9 (sample means/variances over a trajectory segment). All metrics are computed per independent trajectory segment, then aggregated (see \\u00a77).\\n\\n### 5.1 Torsional time series at the fine grid\\n\\nInputs: for each segment of the ground-truth trajectory, the tor t\\u2208{0,\\u2026,T\\u22121}. For the subsampled condition, only \\u03c6_{t_k} at k=0,1,\\u2026,T/N are observed; the \\\"fine grid\\\" values at intermediate t are undefined by construction and an attempt to apply the metrics below to subsampled alone is a Nyquist violation that the experiment labels as such. For MDGEN-augmented, \\u03c6_t at every fine frame is the dihedral of the MDGEN-sampled structure; bonds/backbones are wrapped using whatever convention MDGEN adopts (i.e., dihedral angle convention consistent with the reference MD engine).\\n\\n### 5.2 Autocorrelation (primary)\\n\\nFor each torsional series \\u03c6_t (and analogously \\u03c8_t) on the fine grid,\\n\\n```\\nC_\\u03c6(\\u03c4) = \\u27e8(\\u03c6_t \\u2212 \\u03bc_\\u03c6)(\\u03c6_{t+\\u03c4} \\u2212 \\u03bc_\\u03c6)\\u27e9 / \\u03c3_\\u03c6\\u00b2,  \\u03c4 = 0, 1, \\u2026, \\u03c4_max\\n```\\n\\nwhere \\u27e8\\u00b7\\u27e9 is a time average (and for each segment, separately normalized by that segment's \\u03c3_\\u03c6). Per-segment estimates are computed; cross-segment means and block-bootstrap confidence bands are reported per \\u00a77. Reporting points: \\u03c4 = 0 (\\u22611, sanity), \\u03c4 at the *ground-truth* oscillation half-period, \\u03c4 equal to the coarse stride N\\u00b7\\u0394t_gt (a regime where subsampled alone carries no information between coarse frames), and \\u03c4 up to ~20\\u00d7 the coarse stride.\\n\\n### 5.3 Power spectral density (primary)\\n\\nS_\\u03c6(\\u03c9) is the periodogram of \\u03c6_t \\u2212 \\u03bc_\\u03c6 computed on each segment:\\n\\n```\\nS_\\u03c6(\\u03c9_k) = |\\u03a3_t (\\u03c6_t \\u2212 \\u03bc_\\u03c6) e^{\\u2212i \\u03c9_k t}|\\u00b2 / (T \\u00b7 \\u0394t_aug \\u00b7 \\u03c3_\\u03c6\\u00b2),   \\u03c9_k = 2\\u03c0 k / (T \\u0394t_aug)\\n```\\n\\nwith frequency normalization by \\u03c3_\\u03c6\\u00b2 to make the spectrum dimensionless and comparable across segments. Welch-style segment-averaging (Welch window `[RC]`, segment overlap `[RC]`) is used per segment; cross-segment averaging is then over the resulting per-segment spectra.\\n\\n### 5.4 Oscillation-band integrated power (primary)\\n\\nDefinition of the band is data-driven and pre-registered: from the *ground-truth* spectrum S^gt_\\u03c6(\\u03c9), identify the dominant high-frequency peak above a frequency floor f_min `[RC]` Hz (chosen to lie clearly above the coarse-stride Nyquist frequency), and take the band as \\u03c9 \\u2208 [\\u03c9_peak \\u2212 \\u0394, \\u03c9_peak + \\u0394] where \\u0394 equals one ground-truth FWHM `[RC]` (or an absolute width `[RC]`, whichever is easier to bound). Integrated power in the band is\\n\\n```\\nP_\\u03c6 = \\u222b_{band} S_\\u03c6(\\u03c9) d\\u03c9.\\n```\\n\\nThe per-condition band-integrated power ratio R_\\u03c6 = P_\\u03c6^{condition} / P_\\u03c6^{ground} is the headline number for **C1**.\\n\\n### 5.5 Quantitative lag-window bounds (secondary, paired with qualitative)\\n\\nChoose a lag window \\u03c4 \\u2208 [0, \\u03c4_window] where \\u03c4_window = [RC] \\u00d7 20 \\u00b7 N \\u00b7 \\u0394t_gt `[RC]`. Define\\n\\n```\\n\\u03b5_\\u03c6 = (1/|W|) \\u00b7 \\u222b_{\\u03c4\\u2208W} |C_\\u03c6^{condition}(\\u03c4) \\u2212 C_\\u03c6^{gt}(\\u03c4)| d\\u03c4\\n```\\n\\nand report the ratio (or relative L1) \\u03b5_\\u03c6 / \\u2016C_\\u03c6^{gt}\\u2016_1. Same for \\u03c8. Pre-registered acceptable bound: \\u03b5_\\u03c6 below `[RC]`. This is the quantitative counterpart of \\u00a72 C1/C2 and is the operationalization of \\\"shape agreement within tolerance\\\" required by the qualitative metric.\\n\\n### 5.6 Ramachandran free energy (population-level sanity, **H3**)\\n\\n`F(\\u03c6, \\u03c8) = \\u2212kT ln P(\\u03c6, \\u03c8)`, with P estimated by 2-D histogram binning on the fine grid over a trajectory segment (per-condition, per-segment). Reported as F-condition vs. F-gt over the densely populated region (defined as the set of (\\u03c6, \\u03c8) bins where the ground-truth probability is above a threshold `[RC]`). This is a stratification check and is **explicitly not** a fast-dynamics metric \\u2014 the brief calls it out as a population-level sanity only.\\n\\n### 5.7 Time-reverse bispectrum (control-supporting, optional)\\n\\nFor systems where forward/reverse dynamics might still be detectable, a bispectrum of \\u03c6 and \\u03c8 can flag lost time-asymmetry. Optional, de-prioritized if the autocorrelation evidence is already conclusive.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Baselines and negative controls\\n\\n### 6.1 Baselines (predict smooth interpolation, must fail C1)\\n\\n#### 6.2.1 Linear interpolation (LI)\\n\\nConstruction: between coarse frames \\u03c6_{t_k} and \\u03c6_{t_{k+1}}, \\u03c6_t = \\u03c6_{t_k} + (\\u03c6_{t_{k+1}} \\u2212 \\u03c6_{t_k})(t \\u2212 t_k)/(t_{k+1} \\u2212 t_k). Effective fine-rate \\u03c6 at every integer frame t. Applied to \\u03c6 and \\u03c8.\\n\\n**Predicted behaviour.** C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4) within any one coarse interval is exactly the linear segment's autocorrelation: a single-peaked, segment-monotone shape with no high-frequency oscillation. P_\\u03c6 in the oscillation band \\u2192 0 (or to a numerical-floor noise level determined by the variance across coarse intervals). Test: LI should fail C1 by a wide margin; that margin is the headroom MDGEN must exceed.\\n\\n#### 6.2.2 Cubic-spline interpolation (CI)\\n\\nConstruction: piecewise cubic spline through coarse \\u03c6 values, with derivative boundary conditions per the chosen convention (natural or not-a-knot `[RC]`).\\n\\n**Predicted behaviour.** Within a coarse interval, spline interpolation produces a single smooth segment with at most one local extremum; no oscillation. Marginal improvement over LI in mean squared error, no genuine improvement on oscillation-band power. Test: CI should also fail C1, and the LI-vs-CI gap should be small in the oscillation band (because both fail). This is the joint claim of \\u00a76 \\u2014 both fail, but they fail differently at low frequencies.\\n\\n### 6.3 Generative controls (predict forward-dynamics-sensitive vs not)\\n\\n#### 6.3.3 Time-reversed coarse frames \\u2192 MDGEN (REV)\\n\\nConstruction: take the coarse frames in original order t_0, t_1, \\u2026, t_K. Form the reversed sequence t_K, t_{K\\u22121}, \\u2026, t_0, and run MDGEN bridging on it. The joint distribution of individual coarse frames is identical to the forward case; the temporal order is reversed.\\n\\n**Predicted behaviour.** C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4) is by construction symmetric in \\u03c4 (C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4) = C_\\u03c6(\\u2212\\u03c4)), so REV's autocorrelation at positive lags will approximately match the forward MDGEN's. Therefore REV is only a *weak* control on autocorrelation. **Honest limitation documented:** REV is included for completeness and as a joint check with the optional bispectrum (\\u00a75.7); it should not be relied upon in isolation. The relevant output of this control is to confirm that the primary metric is symmetric in time-reversal (a sanity check that the metric is well-defined).\\n\\n#### 6.3.4 Shuffled coarse frames \\u2192 MDGEN (SHF)\\n\\nConstruction: randomly permute the coarse frame indices; pass the shuffled sequence to MDGEN as if it were a real trajectory.\\n\\n**Predicted behaviour.** Coarse-frame marginal and pairwise statistics are preserved by permutation; the per-segment power spectral density of MDGEN output on a *shuffled* coarse conditioning is predicted to lose definition because MDGEN's bridging depends on consecutive coarse frames. P_\\u03c6 in the oscillation band is predicted to be lower than forward MDGEN if MDGEN genuinely uses temporal conditioning (some residual may remain if MDGEN regenerates fine frames conditioned only on a single coarse frame's neighborhood). Test: SHF must fail C1 to confirm that MDGEN is using the temporal order; otherwise \\u00a72 C3's \\\"not memorization\\\" claim is unsupported.\\n\\n### 6.4 Optional: physics baseline (deferred)\\n\\nA deterministic MD-relaxation or energy minimization between consecutive coarse frames, using the supplied force field. System-dependent cost; for alanine dipeptide it is cheap. Predicted behaviour: produces a smooth relaxation trajectory that should fail C1 by the same logic as LI/CI (no oscillation). Included only if cost is negligible.\\n\\n### 6.5 Attribution logic (updated A3)\\n\\nNo single baseline or control is dispositive. The four baselines/controls are evaluated jointly:\\n\\n- LI and CI together confirm that recovery is *not* a free consequence of any smooth interpolation.\\n- SHF confirms recovery is *not* a free consequence of matching marginal or pairwise structural statistics.\\n- REV is a sanity check that the primary metric is time-symmetric where it should be.\\n\\nC3 (the attribution claim) is judged to hold only if **MDGEN passes C1\\u2013C2 while LI, CI, and SHF each fail C1** (REV is allowed to behave like MDGEN on autocorrelation, as expected).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 7.1 Per-metric comparison procedure\\n\\nFor each trajectory system \\u00d7 stride \\u00d7 independent segment, compute \\u03c6, \\u03c8 spect tra at the fine grid, derive C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4), C_\\u03c8(\\u03c4), S_\\u03c6(\\u03c9), S_\\u03c8(\\u03c9), P_\\u03c6, P_\\u03c8, \\u03b5_\\u03c6, \\u03b5_\\u03c8, F(\\u03c6, \\u03c8), and per-condition band ratios. Aggregate across segments by computing per-system, per-stride sample mean and block-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals.\\n\\n### 7.2 Sample-size sketch\\n\\nPer-segment variabilities of C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4), S_\\u03c6(\\u03c9), and P_\\u03c6 are unknown *a priori*, since no claimable reference numbers exist here. Sketch: for a chosen-effect-on-band-power \\u0394 = (P_\\u03c6^{gen} \\u2212 P_\\u03c6^{shuf}) / \\u03c3, with \\u03c3 estimated from a pilot of `[RC]` independent segments per tier, the standard paired-difference formula at two-sided \\u03b1 = 0.05, power 0.8, gives a per-tier sample size N_seg that is part of the analysis output; the design treats this as an analytic computation rather than a pre-committed number. Sections \\u00a77.3 and \\u00a77.4 turn the sample-size estimate into the actual pre-registration.\\n\\n### 7.3 Statistical tests for paired time series\\n\\n- *Per-lag, per-physical-pair comparison of C_\\u03c6(\\u03c4):* paired block bootstrap (block = coarse-interval for autocorrelation; block = segment for PSD) to produce 95% CIs on the mean difference; report the lag-window CIs as the operationalization of \\u03b5.\\n- *PSD-band power:* paired bootstrap on per-segment integrated power (P_\\u03c6^{condition} \\u2212 P_\\u03c6^{gt}); same for \\u03c8.\\n- *Frequency-localization (C2):* paired bootstrap on the peak frequency \\u03c9_peak derived from each segment's spectrum; the tolerance `[RC]` is reported as a CI on (\\u03c9_peak^{gen} \\u2212 \\u03c9_peak^{gt}).\\n- *Ramachandran (H3):* paired bootstrap on per-binned log-probability differences over the populated bin mask.\\n\\nTests are paired because each MDGEN-augmented segment has a matched ground-truth segment (same trajectory origin, different bridging), so the paired structure is automatic.\\n\\n### 7.4 Pre-registration of what counts as \\\"recovery\\\"\\n\\nA condition \\u00d7 system \\u00d7 stride is judged to **recover** fast torsional dynamics jointly and only if all the following hold on the per-segment paired estimates:\\n\\n1. *C1 \\u2014 oscillation presence:* the lower 95% CI on R_\\u03c6 = P_\\u03c6^{gen} / P_\\u03c6^{gt} in the oscillation band is above `[RC]` (e.g., fraction of ground-truth integrated power) **and** the lower 95% CI on R_\\u03c6 \\u2212 R_\\u03c6^{LI} is positive in the same band (i.e., MDGEN exceeds linear interpolation).\\n2. *C2 \\u2014 frequency localization:* the 95% CI on the difference \\u03c9_peak^{gen} \\u2212 \\u03c9_peak^{gt} contains 0 and is contained within \\u00b1 `[RC]` FWHM (or absolute tolerance).\\n3. *C3 \\u2014 attribution:* LI, CI, and SHF each individually show lower-bound CIs on their R\\u00b7 below `[RC]` in the oscillation band, while MDGEN satisfies (1). REV is reported separately for the symmetry sanity check.\\n\\nAcross tiers the design declares an affirmative answer to the original question if C1\\u2013C3 hold at the anchor stride on T1 (alanine dipeptide, primary) and at minimum on T2; T3 is reported as a contingent extension.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility checks (gates before execution)\\n\\n### 8.1 Binding ground-truth frame-rate constraint (added A7)\\n\\nPre-execution check, must pass before any data acquisition. Compute \\u03c6, \\u03c8 spectrograms on a short pilot of the ground-truth trajectory at the candidate \\u0394t_gt and identify the dominant torsional oscillation period T_osc. Pass if T_osc > `[RC]` \\u00d7 \\u0394t_gt (e.g., \\u00d7 20, comfortably above Nyquist and above any minimum sampling for oscillation frequency resolution). Fail otherwise; in which case the study is not answerable on that dataset at that \\u0394t_gt and a finer-\\u0394t_gt reference is required. Documented as a hard gate rather than a tunable parameter.\\n\\n### 8.2 CPU-vs-GPU cost per tier\\n\\n- **T1 (alanine).** Per coarse-stride fine-rate trajectory segment of length L_gt `[RC]` ps, MDGEN sampling `[RC]` frames between consecutive coarse anchors. Approximate order-of-magnitude cost in CPU-hours/segment is `[RC]` (e.g., 0.1\\u201310) at the most inexpensive `MDGEN` setting consistent with \\u00a79. Aggregate across the stride sweep and `[RC]` segments becomes a wall-clock plan;\\n- **T2 (peptide).** Cost scales roughly with the number of atoms; within reach of the sandbox at `[RC]` segments per stride (TBC after benchmark in \\u00a78.3 below);\\n- **T3 (protein).** Out-of-sandbox; execution requires external compute and the \\u00a79 inputs.\\n\\n### 8.3 Dependency footprint and benchmarking plan\\n\\nLocal feasibility check (per the researcher's scoping decision): a *tiny synthetic two-atom demo* of the metric definitions (LI, CI, autocorrelation, PSD, band-integrated power, paired bootstrap) on simulated noisy torsions, to confirm that the analysis pipeline produces matching C1/C2 results against a known closed-form oscillation + noise mixture. This is the only computation the sandbox is required to execute and is independent of MDGEN availability. Cost: minutes.\\n\\n### 8.4 Other gating checks\\n\\n- Trajectory length sufficient for PSD resolution at the lowest \\u03c9 of interest (FFT resolution \\u0394\\u03c9 = 2\\u03c0 / (T \\u0394t_aug); requirement: oscillation-peak FWHM \\u2265 `[RC]` \\u00d7 \\u0394\\u03c9).\\n- Segment independence assumption (decorrelation of \\u03c6, \\u03c8 across segments over the longest \\u03c4_window sampled); if violated, increase segment length or shorten \\u03c4_window.\\n- Per-condition sample-size estimate (\\u00a77.2) returns a feasible number of segments per tier.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. What the researcher must inject before execution\\n\\nThis section is first-class: the design cannot move to execution without all of the following.\\n\\n| Item | Reason | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| MDGEN package + pinned version | The model is the central object under test. | Specific package + commit/version tag `[RC]`. |\\n| Checkpoint file(s) | Required to actually run inference; \\\"MDGEN\\\" without a checkpoint is undefined. | One per tier; pinned repo + sha `[RC]`. |\\n| System topology + force-field metadata | Defines atoms, bonds, and dihedral convention used for \\u03c6, \\u03c8 extraction. | AMBER/CHARMM/OpenMM/... `[RC]`. |\\n| High-frame-rate reference trajectory(ies) with frame-rate metadata | Needed for C1 ground truth. | \\u0394t_gt must pass \\u00a78.1; T1, T2 mandatory; T3 contingent. |\\n| Chosen stride list | The \\u00a74.3 sweep needs concrete values tied to the checkpoint. | Anchor = training stride of the supplied checkpoint `[RC]`; finer and coarser as proposed in \\u00a74.3 `[RC]`. |\\n| (Implicit) Scientific Python stack + MD analysis lib + pinned versions | Required by \\u00a75 metric pipeline. | mdtraj / MDAnalysis / numpy / scipy `[RC]`. |\\n\\nIf any of T1/T2/T3 cannot be supplied, only the supplied tiers are analysed and that constraint is reported with the results.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Explicit non-goals and out-of-scope items\\n\\n- **No claim of executed experiment.** This document does not assert that any segment has been computed, that any check has passed, that any MDGEN checkpoint has been loaded, or that any number has been measured.\\n- **No MDGEN paper / checkpoint / dataset citation.** No paper title, no checkpoint id, no dataset name, no result is cited. The phrase \\\"MDGEN-style diffusion/flow bridging\\\" is used as a literature concept only.\\n- **T3 (fast-folding proteins) is contingent.** Designed but not executable in the sandbox; reported only if \\u00a79 inputs and external compute are available.\\n- **No claim of which specific numerical recovery threshold is \\\"good enough.\\\"** All thresholds `[RC]` are pre-registration placeholders, not cited standards.\\n- **Out of scope:** whether the recovered fast dynamics are *physically meaningful for downstream observables* (e.g., NMR J-coupling, FRET, infrared spectra inferred from trajectories). These are downstream scientific-application questions that the present design does not adjudicate. Karplus-type J-coupling reconstruction from MDGEN-augmented \\u03c6 distributions is flagged as an exploratory downstream test only, not part of the primary metric battery.\\n- **Out of scope: training, fine-tuning, or retraining of MDGEN.** The design treats the model as a fixed black box defined by its supplied checkpoint.\\n- **Out of scope: comparisons to alternative generative models** (other diffusion / flow / score-based bridges, or other post-hoc upsamplers) unless the researcher explicitly adds them; only the four baselines/controls specified in \\u00a76 are included by default.\\n- **Out of scope: the absent `track.md` content.** Flagged once in \\u00a70; design proceeds without it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A \\u2014 Assumption ledger (post-decision)\\n\\n- **A1.** \\\"Fast dynamics\\\" is operationalized as backbone \\u03c6, \\u03c8 torsional oscillations on timescales much smaller than the MDGEN training stride. *Accepted.*\\n- **A2.** \\u03c6, \\u03c8 autocorrelation functions and their power spectral densities on the recovered fine grid are the primary, falsifiable probes; Ramachandran free energy is a population-level sanity check only. *Accepted.*\\n- **A3.** *Amended (per researcher):* attribution of any recovery is a *joint* claim across baselines and controls \\u2014 specifically, MDGEN passes C1\\u2013C2 while LI, CI, and SHF each fail C1 in oscillation-band power; the joint pattern is the attribution. REV is a weaker sanity check because torsion autocorrelation is approximately symmetric under time reversal.\\n- **A4.** Time-reversed and shuffled-frame MDGEN augmentations are sufficient (jointly with LI/CI) to rule out smooth-interpolation and two-point-distribution-memorization explanations of any recovery. *Accepted, with the joint-claim caveat.*\\n- **A5.** The design document is feasible for alanine dipeptide as a primary tier in the provided 24-CPU / no-GPU sandbox; larger systems are scoped but flagged as out-of-sandbox. *Accepted.*\\n- **A6.** The supplied material fixes only the research question and the design-only framing; all concrete MDGEN/system/checkpoint/metric choices are proposals labelled `[RC]`, not citations of provided source. *Accepted.*\\n- **A7 (added).** The ground-truth frame rate \\u0394t_gt is a binding feasibility constraint: the dominant \\u03c6, \\u03c8 oscillation period must be larger than the saved-frame interval by a comfortable multiple `[RC]` (typically \\u2265 ~20\\u00d7), checked before data acquisition as a hard gate. *Accepted.*\\n\\n## Appendix B \\u2014 Uncertainty ledger (post-decision)\\n\\n- **U1.** MDGEN variant, checkpoint, training stride \\u2014 `[RC]` placeholders only; deferred to \\u00a79 must-inject list.\\n- **U2.** Evaluation systems \\u2014 T1 alanine dipeptide (primary, gating), T2 short unstructured peptide (secondary), T3 fast-folding proteins (contingent), per the researcher's decision.\\n- **U3.** Ground-truth trajectory source \\u2014 external trajectory supplied by researcher; \\u00a78.1 binding frame-rate check must pass before any other analysis.\\n- **U4.** Compute target \\u2014 execution assumed in an external environment (\\u00a79 must-inject); local sandbox used only for the \\u00a78.3 synthetic metric-pipeline sanity check.\\n- **U5.** Quantitative recovery tolerance \\u2014 thresholds `[RC]` in \\u00a77.4 are pre-registration placeholders, refined during analysis once per-segment variability is estimated from a pilot.\\n- **U6.** Subsampling regime \\u2014 sweep (anchor + finer + coarser strides), per the researcher's decision.\\n- **U7.** Documented absence of `track.md` \\u2014 flagged once in \\u00a70; **not a blocker**, treated as a known gap.\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace = `/workspace/task.json` only (verified by `find /workspace -maxdepth 4 -type f`). Contents parsed: question, context that the task is design-only and \\\"do not claim that an experiment was run\\\", and a `documents.track.md` placeholder whose only text is `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` That `track.md` file is not on disk (verified by `find / -maxdepth 3 -iname track.md`, no results). No MDGEN-named artefacts anywhere on the filesystem (`find / -maxdepth 3 -iname \\\"*mdgen*\\\"` no results; no alanine / peptide / trajectory files anywhere; `/home/actor/` empty; `/tmp`, `/data`, `/opt` empty of relevant material). Environment block from the task brief pins: bubblewrap runtime, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24, datasets=[], python=python3, deps not preinstalled. T